    <abstract id="aspace_0f3ff43b7a80ca722cfc937c23cce108">The Collection on European and International Dance Music Culture (dated 1960-2024) consists of materials collected by a single anonymous donor documenting dance music and nightlife culture in Europe and Japan, primarily from the 1970s through the 1990s. The majority of the collection documents music movements and nightlife scenes in the United Kingdom, including acid house, acid jazz, house and garage, rare groove, and soul. The collection also represents more specific geographic and identity-based scenes, including Manchester and LGBTQ+ nightlife. A smaller amount of materials document the Belgium new beat, European house, and Japanese house scenes. Most materials are ephemeral in format: flyers, handbills, member cards, and novelty promotional items. The collection also includes newsletters and photographs. The collection provides significant documentation of art, fashion, Black history, LGBTQ+ nightlife, and the development of DJ culture. Within musicology, the collection shows the emergence of new musical genres, scenes, and subcultures. As performance spaces were often fleeting and in some cases members-only, enduring documentation of these activities is rare.</abstract>
    <langmaterial id="aspace_9dbd0948bbe615e6af8fc4c33a9d8a24">Materials are primarily in English, with a small amount of ephemera in French and Japanese.</langmaterial>
  </did>
  <scopecontent id="aspace_832ecb8fcb6ca2fedf2e09fd7f761dd1">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>The Collection on European and International Dance Music Culture (dated 1960-2024) consists of materials collected by a single anonymous donor documenting dance music and nightlife culture in Europe and Japan, primarily from the 1970s through the 1990s. The majority of the collection documents music movements and nightlife scenes in the United Kingdom, including acid house, acid jazz, house and garage, rare groove, and soul. The collection also represents more specific geographic and identity-based scenes, including Manchester and LGBTQ+ nightlife. A smaller amount of materials document the Belgium new beat, European house, and Japanese house scenes. Most materials are ephemeral in format: flyers, handbills, member cards, and novelty promotional items. The collection also includes newsletters and photographs. The collection provides significant documentation of art, fashion, Black history, LGBTQ+ nightlife, and the development of DJ culture. Within musicology, the collection shows the emergence of new musical genres, scenes, and subcultures. As performance spaces were often fleeting and in some cases members-only, enduring documentation of these activities is rare.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <arrangement id="aspace_c6a9177161fd690e5b68c3b969bcda73">
    <head>Arrangement</head>
<p>Materials are arranged alphabetically by country, maintaining the creator's original groupings and chronological arrangement of materials.</p>  </arrangement>
  <accessrestrict id="aspace_6c1cd09ccdab8b1e9c0afaaaba3a2c91">
    <head>Conditions Governing Access</head>
<p>Materials are open without restrictions.</p>  </accessrestrict>
  <userestrict id="aspace_e9d272965b1e724e4811e56be96c07f9">
    <head>Conditions Governing Use</head>
<p>Because of the assembled nature of this collection, copyright status varies across the collection. Copyright is assumed to be held by the original creator of individual items in the collection; these items are expected to pass into the public domain 120 years after their creation. NYU Special Collections is not authorized to grant permission to publish or reproduce materials from this collection.</p>  </userestrict>
  <acqinfo id="aspace_dc1386d2fb6d2328385b38423d3a07e0">
    <head>Immediate Source of Acquisition</head>
<p>Donated by a single anonymous donor in July 2026; the accession number associated with this gift is 2026.052. The donor wishes to remain anonymous so that the focus of the collection can remain on the materials themselves and the broader context that they document.</p>  </acqinfo>
  <prefercite id="aspace_b6a665bfca62fdcaf4808c5573f95dab">
    <head>Preferred Citation</head>
<p>Identification of item, date; Collection on European and International Dance Music Culture; MSS 669; box number; folder number or item identifier; Fales Library and Special Collections, New York University.</p>  </prefercite>
  <separatedmaterial id="aspace_0520a7063013dcdf91a585a4306f8a38">
    <head>Separated Materials</head>
<p>Materials documenting house music in Canada, Mexico, and San Francisco were separated from this collection and transferred to the Collection on North American Dance Music Culture (MSS 659), also created and purchased from the same anonymous donor.</p>  </separatedmaterial>
  <processinfo id="aspace_39cc36511c8d0f28c38ed9b85210da2f">
    <head>Processing Information</head>
<p>At the time of accessioning materials were removed from their original binders and rehoused in archival boxes and folders, maintaining original groupings of materials (e.g., binders on specific areas or subcultures). Materials were described on the collection-level with a folder-level inventory. With the exception of the folders titled "Multiple Countries," all folder titles are original to the donor and reflect their categorization of the music genres and subcultures documented in the collection. One folded textile was removed from its original location in "United Kingdom -- LGBTQIA+, Alternative -- Binder 2,"  unfolded, and rehoused in a larger folder.</p>  </processinfo>
